'''Jeffrey Alan Combs''' {{Born|9|September|1954}} is an actor who has the distinction of portraying nine different characters on four ''[[Star Trek]]'' television series, most notably those of [[Brunt]] and the various [[Weyoun]]s on {{S|DS9}}, and [[Thy'lek Shran]] on {{S|ENT}}.

Most recently, he voiced [[AGIMUS]] in the {{S|LD}} episodes {{E|Where Pleasant Fountains Lie}}, {{E|A Mathematically Perfect Redemption}} and {{E|A Few Badgeys More}}.

== Personal life ==
Born in Oxnard, California, Combs was raised in Lompoc, California along with many older and younger siblings. He graduated from Lompoc High School in 1972, after which he honed his acting talents at the Pacific Conservatory of the Performing Arts in Santa Maria, California and the Professional Actor's Training Program at the University of Washington in Seattle, Washington.

== Career ==
=== Films ===
In 1980, after spending four years performing for regional theater, Combs moved to Los Angeles where he landed roles in the films ''Whose Life Is It Anyway?'' and ''Honky Tonk Freeway'', both released in 1981. The latter one starred [[Terri Garr]].

Combs is widely recognized as an actor of science fiction and horror movies. His first experience with both genres came in 1983 when he appeared in {{wt|The Man with Two Brains}}, a film which also featured fellow ''Trek'' guest stars [[James Cromwell]], [[David Warner]] and [[Earl Boen]]. A few months later, he was seen in the horror film ''Frightmare'', co-starring [[Scott Thomson]].

Combs has since starred in many movies in the horror and science fiction genres, specifically those based on the works of writer {{W|H. P. Lovecraft}}. His most well-known Lovecraftian role (and, indeed, his most famous film role in general) is that of Dr. Herbert West in the 1985 classic ''Re-Animator''. He reprised this role in two more films, ''Bride of Re-Animator'' in 1990 and ''Beyond Re-Animator'' in 2003.

Other Lovecraftian films starring Combs include ''From Beyond'' (1986, with [[Ted Sorel]]) and ''Lurking Fear'' (1994, with [[Vincent Schiavelli]]). Combs' association with Lovecraft was such that he actually played the author in {{wt|Necronomicon (film)|Necronomicon}} (1993, which featured [[Dennis Christopher]], [[Gary Graham]], [[Richard Lynch]] and David Warner. His more recent Lovecraftian work was the television horror thriller ''The Dunwich Horror'' (2009, with [[Dean Stockwell]]). Combs voiced King Abdul in the animated feature ''Howard Lovecraft & the Undersea Kingdom'' (2017) and H.P. Lovecraft himself in the video sequel ''Howard Lovecraft and the Kingdom of Madness'' (2018).

Outside of the Lovecraftian universe, Combs portrayed a Catholic Cleric in {{wt|The Pit and the Pendulum (1991 film)|The Pit and the Pendulum}} (1991, starring [[Lance Henriksen]], with [[Stephen Lee]]). Combs was further notable for playing the title role of ''Doctor Mordrid'' in 1992, on which he co-starred with [[Brian Thompson]]. Another notable role is that of crazed FBI agent Milton Dammers in {{wt|The Frighteners}} (1996, co-starring [[Julianna McCarthy]].

Combs' other non-Lovecraftian sci-fi and horror films include {{wt|Robot Jox}} (1989, starring Gary Graham), ''Guyver'' (1991, with [[Michael Berryman]], [[Dennis Madalone]], [[Brian Simpson]] and [[Spice Williams]]), {{wt|Trancers II}} (1991, starring [[Tim Thomerson]], with Richard Lynch), {{wt|Fortress (1992 film)|Fortress}} (1992, co-starring [[Kurtwood Smith]] and [[Tom Towles]]), ''I Still Know What You Did Last Summer'' (1998, along with [[Bill Cobbs]]), {{wt|House on Haunted Hill (1999 film)|House on Haunted Hill}} (1999, starring [[Famke Janssen]]), and ''Contagion'' (2002, with [[Megan Gallagher]]). Later credits include {{wt|Abominable (2006 film)|Abominable}} (2006, with [[Matt McCoy]] and [[Phil Morris]]), {{wt|The Wizard of Gore (2007 film)|The Wizard of Gore}} (2007, with [[Brad Dourif]]), and ''Dark House'' (2009, with [[Diane Salinger]] and [[Don Stark]]).

Combs has also appeared in {{wt|Love and a .45}} (1994) and appeared as Gilroy in {{wt|Dillinger and Capone}} (1995, co-starred [[F. Murray Abraham]], [[Stephen Davies]], [[Catherine Hicks]], [[Jeffrey Dean Morgan]], [[Clint Howard]], [[Bert Remsen]] and [[Time Winters]]). Combs was also seen in the thriller ''Felony'' (1996, co-starring [[Charles Napier]] and David Warner), {{wt|Caught Up (film)|Caught Up}} (1998, with [[Tony Todd]]), and {{wt|Edmond (film)|Edmond}} (2005). Additionally, in 1997, Combs and his ''Deep Space Nine'' co-stars [[René Auberjonois]] and [[Armin Shimerman]] appeared together in the drama ''Snide and Prejudice'' (along with [[Mick Fleetwood]]).

Further film work include ''Urgency'' (2010), {{wt|Dorothy and the Witches of Oz}} (2012, with [[Christopher Lloyd]]), {{wt|Night of the Living Dead 3D: Re-Animation}} (2012, with [[Rhonda Aldrich]]), {{wt|Would You Rather (film)|Would You Rather}} (2012, with [[Larry Cedar]] and [[Bobby C. King]]), {{wt|Elf-Man}} (2012), the thriller ''Favor'' (2013), the horror film ''The Penny Dreadful Picture Show'' (2013, with [[Sid Haig]] and [[Brandon Stacy]]), {{wt|Suburban Gothic (film)|Suburban Gothic}} (2014, with [[Ray Wise]]), the family movie ''Beethoven's Treasure'' (2014, with [[Brian George]] and [[Patrick Kwok-Choon]]), the horror comedy ''Art School of Horrors'' (2015), the fantasy film ''Age of Stone and Sky: The Sorcerer Beast'' (2018), and the horror film ''Holiday Hell'' (2018).

He also appeared in {{wt|Unbelievable!!!!!}} in 2018, which features an almost whole ''Star Trek'' cast including [[Chase Masterson]], [[Garrett Wang]], [[Tim Russ]], [[Nichelle Nichols]], [[Robert Picardo]], [[Michael Dorn]], [[Marina Sirtis]], [[Nana Visitor]], [[Walter Koenig]], [[Linda Park]], [[Connor Trinneer]], [[Manu Intiraymi]], [[Dina Meyer]], [[Olivia d'Abo]], [[Julie Warner]], [[Armin Shimerman]], [[John Billingsley]], [[Dominic Keating]], [[Max Grodénchik]], [[Casey Biggs]], [[Brenda Bakke]], [[Patti Yasutake]], [[McKenzie Westmore]], [[Anthony Montgomery]], [[Vaughn Armstrong]], [[Gary Graham]], [[Steve Rankin]], [[Michael Dante]], [[Jack Donner]], [[Michael Forest]], [[Sean Kenney]], [[Gary Lockwood]], [[Barbara Luna]], [[Beverly Washburn]], [[Celeste Yarnall]], [[Bobby Clark]], [[Jasmine Jessica Anthony]], [[Crystal Allen]], [[Menina Fortunato]] and [[Christopher Doohan]].

=== Television ===
Outside of ''Star Trek'', Combs has made guest appearances on several other television series. In 1987, he appeared on an episode of {{wt|Beauty and the Beast (1987 TV series)|Beauty and the Beast}}, starring [[Linda Hamilton]] and [[Ron Perlman]]. Also in 1987, he was seen on ''Houston Knights'', working with [[Madlyn Rhue]].

Combs has also worked on {{wt|The Flash (1990 TV series)|The Flash}} (1991, with [[Dick Miller]]), ''[[Babylon 5]]'' (1994, starring [[Claudia Christian]] and [[Bill Mumy]] and [[David L. Crowley]]), {{wt|Perversions of Science}} (1997, with [[Jeff Corey]], Ron Perlman and David Warner), and the remake of ''The Twilight Zone'' (2003). He also played the recurring role of Kevin Burkhoff on {{wt|The 4400}} (2005-07).

Other series on which he has appeared include {{wt|Freddy's Nightmares}} (1989, starring [[Robert Englund]]), {{wt|Hunter (1984 American TV series)|Hunter}} (1991, starring [[Charles Hallahan]], with [[Kenneth Marshall]][[Ellen Wheeler]] and [[Gene Dynarski]]), {{wt|Life Goes On (TV series)|Life Goes On}} (1991, starring [[Bill Smitrovich]], with [[David Graf]]), ''The Single Guy'' (1996, starring [[Olivia d'Abo]] and [[Mark Moses]]), ''Martial Law'' (2000, with [[Neal McDonough]] and [[T.J. Storm]]),{{wt|CSI: Crime Scene Investigation}} (2003, starring [[Paul Guilfoyle]] and [[Wallace Langham]])  and ''Cold Case'' (2008).

In 1996, Combs had a role in {{wt|Norma Jean & Marilyn}}. Also starring in this movie were [[Ashley Judd]], [[Steven Culp]], [[David Drew Gallagher]], [[Alex Henteloff]] and [[John Rubinstein]]. In 2005, Combs appeared in two made-for-TV horror movies: ''Voodoo Moon'' and ''Hammerhead: Shark Frenzy''.

Combs is also a sought-after voice actor, appearing in numerous animated television shows. He has most prominently worked as a voice actor for animation set in the DC Animated Universe, specifically as The Scarecrow in ''The New Batman Adventures'' (1997, working on the same episode as [[Charles Rocket]]) and The Question in several episodes of {{wt|Justice League (TV series)|Justice League}} (2004-06, alongside [[Clancy Brown]], [[Robert Foxworth]], [[Virginia Madsen]] and Charles Napier). He also voiced the Scarecrow for the video game ''Batman: Rise of Sin Tzu'' (2003), which also featured [[Cary-Hiroyuki]], [[Loren Lester]] and Ron Perlman. Combs voiced the Autobot Ratchet in [[Roberto Orci]] and [[Alex Kurtzman]]'s ''Transformers: Prime'' (2010-13), Viceroy in ''Chadam'' (2010), The Leader in ''Avengers: World's Mightiest Heroes'' (2010-12), Rat King in ''Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles'' (2012-16), and Ratchet in {{wt|Transformers: Robots in Disguise (2015 TV series)|Transformers: Robots in Disguise}} (2016-17).

Further television guest work includes episodes of {{wt|ThunderCats (2011 TV series)|ThunderCats}} (2012, with [[Will Friedle]], [[Kevin Michael Richardson]] and [[Corey Burton]]), ''Femme Fatales'' (2012), ''Criminal Minds'' (2014), {{wt|Ben 10: Omniverse}} (2014, with [[Bumper Robinson]], [[Dee Bradley Baker]], [[Carlos Alazraqui]] and Michael Dorn), {{wt|Gotham (TV series)|Gotham}} (2015, with [[Becky Ann Baker]]), ''Hulk and the Agents of S.M.A.S.H.'' (2015, with [[Fred Tatasciore]] and [[Clancy Brown]]), ''Stan Against Evil'' (2017), and ''Togtone'' (2018).

== ''Star Trek'' ==
Combs auditioned for the role of [[William T. Riker]] on {{S|TNG}}, but lost the part to [[Jonathan Frakes]]. Frakes remembered Combs and cast him as [[Tiron]] while directing the {{S|DS9}} [[DS9 Season 3|third season]] episode {{E|Meridian}}. (''"Hidden File 10", [[DS9 Season 5 DVD]] special feature'')

Prior to playing Tiron, Combs had also auditioned for the role of [[Borath]] in {{E|The Search, Part II}} ([[To_the_Death_(TDF)_(podcast)]])

Combs went on to make several more appearances on ''Star Trek'', guest starring in thirty-one episodes of ''Deep Space Nine'', one episode of {{S|VOY}}, and eleven episodes of {{S|ENT}}. In {{E|The Dogs of War}}, Combs played ''both'' [[Weyoun 8|Weyoun]] and [[Brunt]], becoming one of only a handful of actors ever to play two unrelated characters in the same episode, and the only one credited for playing both characters in that episode. He did it a second time while playing Weyoun and an unnamed holosuite guest in {{E|What You Leave Behind}}, the final episode of ''Deep Space Nine''. He has also appeared in two ''Star Trek'' series finales: {{E|What You Leave Behind}} and {{E|These Are The Voyages...}}.

He is one of only six actors to play seven or more different characters in the ''Star Trek'' franchise, the others being [[Randy Oglesby]], [[J.G. Hertzler]], [[Vaughn Armstrong]], [[Thomas Kopache]], and [[Brent Spiner]].

The species that he played on ''Star Trek'' include three [[Ferengi]], a [[Vorta]], a [[Norcadian]], and an [[Andorian]]. He also played an imaginary [[Human]] in {{E|Far Beyond the Stars}}. The name of Tiron's race was never revealed. Combs has said that out of all the ''Trek'' roles he has played, Weyoun is his favorite character. {{YouTube|type=v|FjA-hvKiCk0}}

When interviewed in {{STM|43}} in {{Y|1998}} about his two recurring roles on ''Star Trek: Deep Space Nine'', Combs said that he preferred Weyoun chiefly because he had much more "freedom" to define the character with each appearance, whereas playing [[Brunt]] (or any Ferengi character) was simply a matter of following [[Armin Shimerman]]'s example.

[[Manny Coto]] has also said, had ''Star Trek: Enterprise'' been given a [[Undeveloped Star Trek: Enterprise episodes#Proposed fifth season episodes|fifth season]], the recurring character of Shran may have joined ''Enterprise'' as an "auxiliary or an advisor". {{EL|trektoday.com/news/271105_01.shtml}} {{TrekMovie.com|2009/08/10/vegascon09-braga-coto-talk-enterprise-season-5-star-trek-2009-more/}} As of 2018, Combs remarked, he never heard of such a proposal. (''"Behind the Masks" panel at Destination Star Trek Birmingham, 21 October 2018'')

