'''Marc Lawrence''' {{born|17|February|1910|died|28|November|2005}} was a character actor who guest-starred as [[Volnoth]] in the {{s|TNG}} [[TNG Season 3|third season]] episode {{e|The Vengeance Factor}} and [[Carl Zeemo]] in the {{s|DS9}} [[DS9 Season 7|seventh season]] episode {{e|Badda-Bing, Badda-Bang}}.

Lawrence appeared in over two hundred films and television series dating back to 1932. His brooding, sinister appearance often got him cast in the role of villains, especially gangsters and mob bosses &ndash; roles which he played throughout his entire career, from his uncredited appearance in 1932's ''If I Had a Million'' to the 1996 TV movie ''Gotti''. He even played three different gangster roles on ''[[The Untouchables]]''. He was not always cast in such roles, however; he received much praise for his performance as a mute in {{wt|The Shepherd of the Hills (1941 film)|The Shepherd of the Hills}} (1941, starring [[John Wayne]]). However, he is probably best remembered for playing Kloppman in {{wt|Newsies}} (1992, featuring [[Frank Novak]] and [[Kevin Tighe]]).

His extensive film credits also include {{wt|Johnny Apollo (film)|Johnny Apollo}} (1940, with [[Anthony Caruso]]), {{wt|This Gun for Hire}} (1942, starring [[Alan Ladd]], with [[Richard Webb]]), {{wt|The Ox-Bow Incident}} (1943), {{wt|Dillinger (1945 film)|Dillinger}} (1945, with [[Elisha Cook]] and [[Lawrence Tierney]]), {{wt|Cloak and Dagger (1946 film)|Cloak and Dagger}} (1946), {{wt|Key Largo (film)|Key Largo}} (1948), ''The Asphalt Jungle'' (1950, with Anthony Caruso), {{wt|Helen of Troy (film)|Helen of Troy}} (1956, directed by [[Robert Wise]]), {{wt|Custer of the West}} (1967, with [[Jeffrey Hunter]] and Lawrence Tierney), {{wt|Krakatoa, East of Java}} (1969, with [[Brian Keith]]), {{wt|Marathon Man (film)|Marathon Man}} (1976, with [[Fritz Weaver]]), {{wt|Goin' Coconuts}} (1978, with [[Kenneth Mars]] and [[Ted Cassidy]]), {{wt|Hot Stuff (1979 film)|Hot Stuff}} (1979, with [[Raymond Forchion]]), {{wt|The Big Easy (film)|The Big Easy}} (1987), {{wt|Blood Red (film)|Blood Red}} (1989, with [[John de Lancie]]), {{wt|Newsies}} (1992, with [[Frank Novak]]), {{wt|From Dusk till Dawn}} (1996), and ''End of Days'' (1999, with [[Mark Margolis]] and [[Jack Shearer]]). 

He also appeared in {{wt|Diamonds Are Forever (film)|Diamonds Are Forever}} (1971, with [[Sid Haig]] and [[Dick Crockett]]) and {{wt|The Man with the Golden Gun (film)|The Man with the Golden Gun}} (1974), three Abbott and Costello movies (''Hold That Ghost'' in 1941, ''Hit the Ice'' in 1943, and ''Abbott & Costello in the French Foreign Legion'' in 1950), and in three ''Charlie Chan'' films (each playing a different character): ''Charlie Chan on Broadway'' (1937, with [[Keye Luke]]), ''Charlie Chan in Honolulu'' (1938), and ''Charlie Chan at the Wax Museum'' (1940).

His last film appearance came in 2003, when he and fellow ''[[Star Trek]]'' alumni [[Robert Picardo]], [[Ron Perlman]], and [[George Murdock]] played Acme Vice Presidents in ''Looney Tunes: Back in Action''. [[Dick Miller]] also appeared in this film.

Lawrence's [[birth date]] of February 1910 makes him the earliest-born actor to have appeared in ''Deep Space Nine'' (not including the archival footage of actor [[Whit Bissell]] from {{e|The Trouble with Tribbles}} that appeared in the ''Deep Space Nine'' episode {{e|Trials and Tribble-ations}}).

