'''James Joseph Sloyan''' {{born|24|February|1941|died|22|July|2026}} was an actor who played four different characters in {{s|TNG}}, {{s|DS9}}, and {{s|VOY}}.

One of Sloyan's earliest feature film credits was {{wt|The Sting}} (1973, featuring ''Next Generation'' and ''Voyager'' guest [[Ray Walston]]). He also played the role of Barnard "Barney" Smith in the 1979 ''Buck Rogers in the 25th Century'' two-parter "The Plot to Kill a City" (with [[Frank Gorshin]], [[Felix Silla]], [[Anthony James]], [[Seamon Glass]], and [[Tim O'Connor]]). In 1970, he appeared in {{wt|The Traveling Executioner}} with [[Graham Jarvis]], [[Marianna Hill]], [[Stefan Gierasch]], [[Logan Ramsey]], and [[James Greene]].

Sloyan had a role in {{wt|Centennial (miniseries)|Centennial}} (1978, featuring [[Michael Ansara]], [[Cliff DeYoung]], [[Brian Keith]], [[Sally Kellerman]], [[Morgan Woodward]], and [[Anthony Zerbe]]). He co-starred with [[Robert Picardo]] in the 1981 made-for-TV movie ''The Violation of Sarah McDavid'' and guested on {{wt|Ryan's Hope}} (1983). Sloyan's voice was familiar to many as the spokesman for Lexus.

Sloyan made several appearances as Avery Baltus on {{wt|Party of Five}}. He played Dr. Frank Nollette in ''[[The X-Files]]'' (1994, starring [[Gillian Anderson]]). He also guest-starred in an episode of {{wt|Quantum Leap (1989 TV series)|Quantum Leap}} (1991, starring [[Scott Bakula]] and [[Dean Stockwell]]), and appeared as Superintendent Hazen on several episodes of {{wt|Dr. Quinn, Medicine Woman}}, starring [[Barbara Babcock]], as well as recurring on ''Murder, She Wrote'' as insurance investigator Robert Butler, sharing every episode with [[Hallie Todd]].

